/target
/dist
/dist-papers
# Output-isolated dirs for the publish-* family so it runs concurrently with
# tauri-run without clobbering dist/ + target/ (Makefile DIST/TARGET_DIR).
# **A GLOB, not an enumeration.** This list used to name each publish output
# directory one at a time, so every NEW one was committable by default — and
# that is exactly how one 2026-08-21 chat fix swept in 18,408 files: a 114 MB
# `dist-all/` publish tree plus an 11 MB frozen SPA shell, neither of which any
# target regenerates under those names. Same shape as the cache-header rule this
# repo already learned: when a policy enumerates the exceptions and defaults the
# rest to the unsafe value, everything added later inherits the unsafe value
# silently. Nothing under a `dist-` prefix is source; if that ever stops being
# true, negate the one path rather than going back to a list.
/dist-*
/dist-publish
/dist-federation
/dist-registry
# The uploadable production web tree (`make site-dist` / SITE_DIST_OUT). Built,
# never committed — and deliberately NOT under dist/, which trunk wipes.
/dist-site
/target-publish
# Transient staging dirs for `make tauri-bundle` — the recipe copies an external
# APPS_DIST / INGEST here so the publish container (which mounts only this repo)
# can see them, then removes them. Never committed.
/.apps-stage
/.ingest-stage
# Durable publisher identity (Makefile PUBLISH_DATA_DIR / ENTITY_DATA_DIR) — a
# private keypair; NEVER commit it. The glob matters: the registry emitter
# resolves a SEPARATE durable identity (`{data-dir}-registry/`, so that a
# name-issuer key is not also a content-publisher key), and `/.entity-publish`
# alone does not match `/.entity-publish-registry` — which duly turned up
# untracked in `git status` after a suite run.
/.entity-publish*
# Cargo.lock is COMMITTED (both of them). This repo ships binaries, not a
# library, and a release build must be reproducible: without the lockfile the
# same tag resolves different transitive versions on different days. Un-ignored
# 2026-08-17 as part of the release runway (RELEASE-READINESS §8).
src-tauri/target/

# Per-machine podman resource-cap overrides (RESOURCE-CAPS.md §4a) — never committed.
caps.local.mk

# Materialized kernel pins (`CORE_RUST_REF=<ref>` → tools/core-pin.sh). Each is a
# `git archive` export of one entity-core-rust commit, mounted over the sibling
# path inside the build container. Built from the sibling's object store, never
# source, and reproducible from a commit — so it is cache, not work. ~13 MB per
# distinct commit; `make core-pin-clean` empties it.
/.core-pin
# (Pins now live outside the tree, ~/.cache/entity-browser-core-pin — kept so an old one is not committed.)
# Cargo build dirs for pinned builds. UNBOUNDED IN THREE AXES, which is why the
# glob has two stars AND no leading slash:
#   1. TARGET_DIR is overridden per-target (`target-publish` for site-serve,
#      `$(SITE_DIST_TARGET)` for site-dist) — so `/target-pin-*` alone is wrong
#      and was: `target-publish-pin-e6213f1` turned up untracked on the first
#      pinned release build.
#   2. The sha varies per pin.
#   3. **The DIRECTORY varies** — `src-tauri` is its own cargo root with its own
#      target dir (ignored on its own line above), so a pinned `make test-tauri`
#      leaves `src-tauri/target-pin-{sha}/`. An anchored pattern cannot match a
#      nested path, so that one stayed untracked. Found 2026-09-09 during a
#      release audit.
#      **It does NOT break `make site-dist`** — checked rather than assumed, and
#      the first draft of this note said it did. `build-pair --check` tests
#      `git diff --quiet HEAD`, which is tracked-only, so an untracked directory
#      never makes the pair read `-dirty`. What it does cost is `git status`
#      readability, which is what this repo's own "report the state before you
#      claim it" rule leans on — and any future check spelled `--porcelain`
#      WOULD trip on it, since that one counts untracked.
# Un-anchored rather than enumerating the two known roots: enumerating would
# default every future one to committable, which is how a 2026-08-21 fix swept in
# 18,408 files (see the dist- note above).
target*-pin-*
.tauri-home/
.cache/

# Python bytecode from tooling scripts
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Release artifacts (make dist / dist-web) — built, never committed.
/artifacts

# Local dev TLS (make serve TLS=1). Contains an unencrypted CA private key that
# test devices are told to trust — machine-local, never committed, never shared.
/.dev-certs/

# Git worktrees, kept IN-REPO so they stop scattering among the sibling
# repos (four stale ones were sitting beside entity-core-rust holding 37 GB).
# Safe here because every linter and census is rooted at `src/` -- measured:
# `find src` counts are identical with a worktree present. A future linter
# rooted at `.` would break that, which is the thing to check before adding one.
# Proposal: docs/plans/PROPOSAL-2026-09-11-b-WORKTREES-*.md
/.worktrees

# Per-contributor / per-session local agent context (ADR-0020). Injectable,
# never committed, never shared. This repo had no entry for it, so the
# ecosystem standard was one `git add` away from being violated by accident.
AGENTS.local.md
/.agents/
